    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d284-a2">

                <pb facs="0677" n="581" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg581"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No. 190.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Port au Prince, Hayti</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-07-31"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 31,
                        1879</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch, dated Le Government
                    Provisoire, Port au Prince, July 29, 1879 and marked “Section of foreign
                    relations,” in which you advise me of the institution of a provisional
                    government by decree, of the 26th instant, of the central revolutionary
                    committee sitting at the capital, and containing two copies of such decree, has
                    just now been received. You will be pleased to accept my very sincere thanks
                    therefor.</p>
                <p>I need not assure you, sirs, that the government which I have the honor to
                    represent at this capital is profoundly interested in everything which concerns
                    the welfare of this republic, and I may, therefore, express with confidence the
                    hope that good understanding may ever subsist between yours and my country.</p>
                <p>With sentiments of exalted consideration. I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN MERCER LANGSTON</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Messrs. <hi rendition="#small-caps">H. Hérissé</hi> and <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">Jh. Lamothe.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
